				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">Upon killing the Arch Liche of the serpent temple that resides far out on the cold airless side of Luclin, the guild’s officers deemed the evening’s raid over and released all members from their active duty to pursue each one’s own interests for the remainder of the night. 
<br />

<br />
While waiting for the Loot Council to decide which deserving members were to receive the treasures that the dead snake possessed, Wraug felt a heavy jab to his side which nearly knocked him over. Turning to strike the fool that assaulted him, he saw that it was only that fat ogre, Mattaru. Not knowing his own strength, Mattaru was confused when Wraug turned and glared at him for the simple nudge he gave to get the monk’s attention. 
<br />

<br />
“What do ye want, ye fat ogre?”, Wraug growled. Then he watched the shadowknight’s facial features repeatedly switch from a slight grin to a blank stare, knowing that it meant that the ogre was contemplating whether to attack him or not. Loosing patience with the ogre’s amazingly slow thought process and indecision, Wraug repeated his question, “What do ye want?”
<br />

<br />
“Um…du, um…um…oh, um…uh, um…oh yaw…uh, did ya keel dat king dwof dat ya says ya wanna keel yet? 
<br />

<br />
Stepping back a couple of paces to wipe the ogre spit off of himself, Wraug replied,” Nay…I have come close a couple o’times, but that dwarven king is a coward and summons his captain of the guard to his side to fight and heal him during battle…one of these days I shall prevail!”
<br />

<br />
Mattaru then asked, “Ya wants my help ta keel em?”
<br />

<br />
Wraug smiled at Mattaru. He never thought that he would consider an ogre to be a friend, but Mattaru’s pure and honest desire to kill and eat dwarves had earned him Wraugs respect many moons ago. Wraug replied, “Sure thing ye fat ogre…ye can eat the guard captain and as many dwarves as ye like, but do not touch the king…for that swine is to die by my hand alone!”
<br />

<br />
Mattaru cheered and began to cackle and snort with excitement. Wraug noticed the ogre was starting to draw attention from others in the guild. Wraug quickly scolded Mattaru and told him to control himself and keep their plans quiet so that the dwarf lovers in the guild don’t send warning of their intentions to the midget king. 
<br />

<br />
Upon being translocated by one of the wizards in the guild, Wraug and Mattaru made their rendezvous in Butcherblock Mountains.  Stating the obvious, Mattaru announced that the dwarves don’t like him as he chanted some dark spell to render himself invisible. “Ha…you nos sees me”, Mattaru blurted. Wraug responded, “Hold yer tongue fool, for with the enchantments of my gear…I can see ye plain as day. Now lets make haste before we draw attention to ourselves.” Mattaru had a look of disappointment on his face as he followed Wraug down the path towards the entrance of the dwarven city. 
<br />

<br />
From a distance they could see the gigantic figure of an evil dwarf carved out of the mountain side that marked the entrance to Kaladim. Wraug began to wish that he had studied the art of wizardry, so that he could use the powerful spells they possessed to burn and carve the stone and transform the figure into a severed dwarven head stuck upon a pig pole. 
<br />

<br />
As Wraug approached the cave entrance to the city, he could smell the combined stank of stale ale, smoke, and refined ore. The stench reminded him of how foul these creatures are to live underground in this polluted state. At least the gnomes, with their high intelligence, devise a means to harness the energy of the underground river that flows through Akanon to power their machines and light sources. Thus the air in the gnomes underground city is clean and pure, unlike the eye irritating, nose bleeding stench of Kaladim. 
<br />

<br />
As he entered the outer gate of the city, the fattest of the four guards that stood there shouted, “Wat ye plan ter be doin here in Kaladim?” 
<br />

<br />
Wraug stopped and glared down at the fat evil midget and calmly stated, “I am here to assassinate your King.” 
<br />

<br />
The dwarves all looked at each other and suddenly broke out into a hysterical laughter. A couple even dropped their weapons, which clanged loudly as they hit the ground. One of them even had tears flowing profusely from his eyes as he laughed, which caused the other three to laugh even harder. In any other city, they would have arrested him or killed him on sight, but these foolish dwarves just laugh. 
<br />

<br />
Wraug gasped as he was completely dumbfounded to see Mattaru kneeling down with one arm on a dwarf (still invisible of course) laughing just as hysterical as those evil little dwarves were. Then he watched in utter amazement as Mattaru, still laughing, slapped the dwarf on the back, causing the midget to fly across the hall and crash into the wall. Wraug thought for sure that the battle was to begin here, but the dwarf merely stood up (still laughing and blubbering like a little girl) and walked back over to his post. The fool seemed to have no clue or care that some invisible force just launched him across the room. Wraug just closed his eyes and shook his head in disgust.
<br />

<br />
The fattest dwarf then began to mumble and sputter, “Ariht, if ye don’t want ter tell us wat yer doin…den ye don’t haf to. Twas de best laf we had in a while tho.” 
<br />

<br />
Wraug just walked past them down the hall leading deeper into the mountain. Once around the corner and out of sight of the guards, Wraug gave Mattaru an icy glare. The ogre just shrugged as if saying, what did I do?
<br />

<br />
Wraug made his way to the palace, of which was swarming with many young guards and knights in training. As he made his way inside the palace, a young knight attempted to stand his ground and stated that no outsiders shall pass. Wraug scowled at the dwarf and said, “Move out of my way swine for I shall soon kill ye then say another word to ye.” The young dwarf refused to move out of his way. Wraug looked at the dwarf and said, “Well, I atleast gave ye a choice!” Then with three quick strikes of his staff, Wraug slayed the guard where he stood as the other young dwarves shrieked and stepped away from him. He took the dead midget’s axe and removed his head from his corpse, meanwhile hearing the invisible ogre’s giggling drown out the cries and squeals of the spectating dwarfs. 
<br />

<br />
King Kazen Stormhammer sat upon his thrown, content in believing that he was the most wealthy and powerful dwarf in all of Norath. He sat there, all fat and greasy like… watching the young knights and warriors that were there to guard and serve him. He began to fantasize about inviting a couple of the young male squires back to his private chamber to have fun and frolic with. Then suddenly, the entrance to his thrown room burst open. From the dark hallway beyond the room, an object flew in and landed in the center of the room. It was the decapitated head of one of his young knights. 
<br />

<br />
In a fit of rage he grabbed for his weapons and stood up, ready to kill whomever committed this ungodly act. Then the dwarf’s blood turned cold as he saw that bald human enter his thrown room, again. Twice already this human has nearly succeeded in taking King’s life, and twice the King has been deceived into thinking that he had slayed him…to only watch the human stand up and run out while his pathetic guards do nothing.  
<br />

<br />
Wraug announced, “Greetings to ye again, ye evil midget King. To remove the curse of the demon dwarf that I have managed to lock away in the dungeons of me brain, I must find a way to wipe out the dwarven race. I believe that this quest can only begin by spilling yer blood. Without a king, the dwarves will be lost…and with all hope, there will be a civil war within the dwarven community and ye evil midgets will wipe the majority of each other out. The few of ye that survive the famine of yer pathetic little war should be easily found and destroyed by myself and my mates. As ye can see, its nothing personal…its just that yer the dwarven king and thus, ye must die!”
<br />

<br />
The King spat his tobacco out on the ground and replied, “Yer mad…and dis time yer corpse shall be incinerated to make no mistake if yer dead!”
<br />

<br />
Wraug nodded and then lunged at the dwarven king, knocking him back out of his thrown. 
<br />

<br />
“Guards!...Guards! Come to my side and protect yer King!” the pompous dwarf shouted. All of the guards and knights in the thrown room just stood back and stared, still startled and shaken by their comrades head laying on the floor. 
<br />

<br />
Then from the back rooms of the palace came the Captain of the Guards. He began to attack the human he saw fighting his King, but noticed that his King was taking a heavy beating. So he stepped back and began chanting a heal spell to place upon his King. 
<br />

<br />
Just before he could murmur the final incantations of the spell, the Captain was knocked across the room into mass of spectating dwarfs. Upon standing to his feet, the Captain could not believe his eyes, for he saw an ogre in the middle of the thrown room…this was no ordinary ogre for it was Mattaru. 
<br />

<br />
What Mattaru failed to disclose to Wraug before joining him on this journey, was that Mattaru was somewhat famous among the dwarves. For during the ogre’s youthful years, he spent a considerable amount of time in Butcherblock feeding on the dwarven guards at the various outposts. Many of those guards being the fathers and uncles to the now young guards and knights present in the palace.  
<br />

<br />
The mere sight of Mattaru enraged a majority of the young dwarves and the taste of revenge drove the fear out of them all. As Wraug was busy giving the dwarven king the beating of his life, he could see swarms of dwarves piling upon the poor ogre. They were up on his back and head, they dangled from his arms and weapon. As bodies would fly from every blow the ogre delivered, fresh bodies fell in to take their place. Wraug screamed as he saw his friend die to the mass of dwarves. 
<br />

<br />
Wraug growled, “Now you shall die pig vomit!” Wraug unleashed devastating blows upon the king as he felt his inner flame discipline ignite within him. Even the heals being delivered from the Captain of the Guard were no match for the damage that Wraug was delivering to the now blood soaked king. 
<br />

<br />
“Come help yer King ye fools!”, shouted the Captain. But mass of dwarves just stood there watching in disbelief. 
<br />

<br />
Then with one last massive blow, everyone in the room heard the bones shatter in their King’s neck. Kazen Stormhammer’s corpse fell to the floor like a bloody sack of potatoes. The Captain, blinded by hate, continued to attack Wraug. One on one, the Captain was no match for the monk. Within short time, Wraug had disposed of the Captain and eyed the crowd of sobbing dwarfs to make sure there were no foolish heroes among them. 
<br />

<br />
Wraug returned to the King and thanked Quellious for his victory. Unfortunately, Wraug could not sense his god’s presence. Instead he shivered slightly as he swore that he felt evil smile of Innoruuk upon him. Wraug new he must quickly get out of Kaladim and return to the Plane of Tranquility where he could enter a deep meditative state and contemplate his actions and the resultant feelings he was experiencing. 
<br />

<br />
Thinking that since he just killed a King, perhaps he would now have some platinum to fill his bank and live comfortably on. But the stinking corpse of the King only possessed a cheap axe, shield, and a mitheral earing. Some mighty king this was, he thought to himself. 
<br />

<br />
Wraug then heard one of the young guards order for someone to go and summon a cleric to resurrect their beloved king. Wraug rushed the fool and asked the midget if he had a death wish. The dwarf just stood there shaking and sobbing. Wraug knelt down to the dwarf’s face and quietly said, “Perhaps I shall stuff ye in a sack and take ye with me. Then, once I get my mate resurrected… he will have a nice tasty snack to munch on while he recovers. How does that sound to ye?”  All that could be heard was the hollow percussion sound of the dwarf as he stood there crapping his armor. 
<br />

<br />
Disgusted with the foul creature, Wraug rolled his eyes and stood up. He went over to Mattaru’s corpse  and mourned the loss of his brave companion. He then dragged the ogre’s corpse and hid it near the city entrance so that he could have a cleric come and resurrect his friend. Wraug then set off to attempt to find forgiveness from his God.  
<br />
